Unqualified names are resolved against search_path, in order:
SHOW search_path; -- "$user", public
SET search_path TO analytics, public;
SELECT * FROM daily_revenue; -- resolves to analytics.daily_revenue
-- persist for a role or a database
ALTER ROLE app_user SET search_path TO app, public;
ALTER DATABASE shop SET search_path TO app, public;
"$user" expands to a schema named after the current role, if one exists — a tidy per-user sandbox pattern. pg_catalog is implicitly searched first, which is why SELECT now() works without qualification.
Watch out (PostgreSQL 15+): the public schema is no longer writable by every user. Since PG 15, CREATE on public is revoked from PUBLIC and the schema is owned by the database owner. Scripts written against PG 14 or earlier that create tables as a non-owner role will fail with permission denied for schema public. Grant explicitly: GRANT CREATE ON SCHEMA public TO app_user;
Common pitfall: a mutable search_path inside SECURITY DEFINER functions is a privilege-escalation vector. Always pin it: CREATE FUNCTION ... SECURITY DEFINER SET search_path = pg_catalog, public.

-- session only, reverts on disconnect
SET work_mem = '64MB';
RESET work_mem;
-- persist cluster-wide: writes postgresql.auto.conf
ALTER SYSTEM SET work_mem = '32MB';
SELECT pg_reload_conf(); -- enough for sighup settings
ALTER SYSTEM SET shared_buffers = '2GB';
-- restart required; check whether one is pending:
SELECT name, setting, pending_restart FROM pg_settings WHERE pending_restart;
-- undo an ALTER SYSTEM
ALTER SYSTEM RESET work_mem;
ALTER SYSTEM RESET ALL;
-- scoped defaults
ALTER DATABASE shop SET statement_timeout = '30s';
ALTER ROLE reporting SET work_mem = '256MB';
Trick: ALTER SYSTEM is the right tool for infrastructure automation because it is transactional-ish, auditable, and survives config-management overwrites of postgresql.conf. But it is also the first place to look when a setting "mysteriously" ignores what postgresql.conf says — postgresql.auto.conf wins.

ALTER SYSTEM SET log_min_duration_statement = '500ms'; -- log slow queries
ALTER SYSTEM SET log_checkpoints = on;
ALTER SYSTEM SET log_lock_waits = on;
ALTER SYSTEM SET log_line_prefix = '%m [%p] %q%u@%d ';
ALTER SYSTEM SET track_io_timing = on; -- needed for EXPLAIN (BUFFERS) timings
ALTER SYSTEM SET shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_stat_statements'; -- restart required
SELECT pg_reload_conf();
pg_stat_statements is the single highest-value thing to enable before you need it — it is the workload profiler that Post 04 leans on entirely.

Install PostgreSQL 17 or 18 by any route above. From psql, report the server version, the data directory, and the config file path in one query each.
Solution
SELECT version();
SHOW data_directory;
SHOW config_file;

Create a database course, a schema staging inside it, and a table staging.import_log(id, loaded_at, rows_loaded). Confirm that \dt does not list it by default, then make it visible without qualifying the name.
Solution
CREATE DATABASE course;
\c course
CREATE SCHEMA staging;
CREATE TABLE staging.import_log (
id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
loaded_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
rows_loaded integer NOT NULL
);
\dt -- empty: staging is not in search_path
SET search_path TO staging, public;
\dt -- now listed

Create a login role analyst that can connect to course and SELECT from every current and future table in public, but cannot write.
Solution
CREATE ROLE analyst LOGIN PASSWORD 'analyst-pw';
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE course TO analyst;
\c course
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO analyst;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO analyst;
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO analyst;
Alternatively, on PG 14+: GRANT pg_read_all_data TO analyst;

